gic that uses these duration values.\n\nChecking the fugit Duration arithmetic...\n\nI need to extract the raw tick values to work with bit shifts, since Duration doesn't directly support those operations. I'll convert to ticks, do the interval calculations on the raw numbers, then compare against the target interval's tick value to verify it falls within the acceptable range. I need to figure out how to extract the raw tick values from fugit's `Instant` type—it looks like I can't directly call `.ticks()` on an `Instant`, so I'll need to use the duration methods and access the underlying value differently. Writing the assertion logic...\n\nFor the congestion algorithm part, I need to figure out how to handle the `ccalgo` field in the spec struct since `get_congestion_algorithm` is still a todo. I could use an `Option` type and unwrap it during the test, or I could just let it panic when the function is called—either way works for now since this is test code.\n\nActually, I'll just define the struct with `ccalgo` as a `&'static CongestionAlgorithm` reference and call `get_congestion_algorithm(\"cubic\")` directly in the test initialization, letting it panic if needed.\n\nNow I need to figure out what imports `TestTlsApiCtx` requires in util.rs. I'm adding `Quic` and `Version` to the existing imports from `crate::internal`, and I'll need `TransportParameters` from `crate::tp`. For the time simulation, I'm setting up an `Instant` starting at zero ticks and planning the duration arithmetic from there.\n\nWriting the test implementation... Setting up the congestion algorithm for both server and client connections, then configuring the network link parameters like latency and bandwidth constraints based on the test specification.\n\nNow I'm enabling binary logging on both sides of the connection and initializing the client connection to start the handshake, then running the connection loop to establish the full TLS connection.\n\nOnce the client is ready, I'm setting up the test scenario with the acknowledgment frequency parameters and running the data sending loop to transmit the test data, then verifying the transmission succeeded against the expected specification.\n\nNow I'm checking that both the client and server successfully negotiated the ack frequency extension, and validating that the negotiated ack delay and gap values match what was specified in the test.\n\nThen I'm calculating the actual packet interval from the server's received packets and comparing it against the expected target interval with a 25% tolerance band. Finally, I'm cleaning up the test context and returning the result.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to port this to Rust—the C pattern of using `ret = -1` for failure and chaining checks with `if (ret == 0)` maps naturally to Rust's `Result` type, where I can use the `?` operator for propagation and `assert!` for invariant checks. Since this is a helper function rather than a test itself, I should return `Result<(), crate::Error>`.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":413,"cache_read_input_tokens":71045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":413},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8527e90-2f36-45ce-b310-d1a913164cfe","uuid":"35e886bf-d253-4b7b-b845-f87733685714"}
